# C

Apply for C translation units and C headers. C++ sources follow `code-cpp`.
When a `.h` is clearly a C++ header (templates, namespaces, `.hpp` peers),
prefer `code-cpp`.

## Format and build

- Match the project's C standard (`-std=c11` / `c17` / …) and warning set.
  Do not raise the language mode for one file.
- Format with the repo's formatter (`clang-format` or the project's
  equivalent). Do not hand-align beyond what the formatter keeps.
- Fix new compiler warnings on code you change. Do not cast away a warning
  without fixing the cause unless the project already suppresses that
  warning for the same reason.
- Prefer the C standard library and the project's existing dependencies.
  Do not add a library for a helper already available.

## Types and memory

- Prefer fixed-width types from `<stdint.h>` for wire formats and sizes that
  must not change across platforms.
- Every `malloc` / `calloc` / `realloc` / `strdup` path has a matching
  free on every exit. Prefer a single cleanup label (`goto cleanup`) over
  duplicated frees when the function has several early returns.
- Check every allocation and I/O result. Do not ignore `NULL` or negative
  return codes on a path a caller can handle.
- Do not use VLAs when the project forbids them. Prefer a bounded heap
  buffer or a fixed stack array with an explicit limit.
- Prefer `size_t` for object counts and sizes. Do not mix signed lengths
  with `size_t` without an explicit bounds check.

## Headers and APIs

- Headers are include-guarded (`#ifndef` / `#define` / `#endif` or
  `#pragma once` when the project already uses it).
- Keep public headers minimal. Do not pull heavy implementation headers into
  a public `.h` when a forward declaration would do.
- Prefer opaque structs at API boundaries when the project already hides
  implementation that way.
- Document ownership in the API: who allocates, who frees, and whether a
  returned pointer may be `NULL`.

## Safety

- Bound every copy and format into a buffer (`snprintf`, `strlcpy` /
  project helpers). Do not use unbounded `strcpy` / `sprintf` /
  `gets`.
- Validate lengths before `memcpy` / `memmove`. Treat untrusted input as
  hostile.
- Do not cast away `const` to mutate shared data. Prefer a mutable copy or
  an API that takes non-const.
- Threaded code follows the project's locking rules. Do not invent a second
  synchronization style for one call.

## Tests

- Prefer the project's C test stack (CTest, Check, Unity, or custom). Ship
  a unit test for a new exported behavior in the same change
  (`code-unit-testing`).
- Cover the failure path (allocation / I/O error) and one success path.
- Do not sleep to wait for a condition. Use the project's existing timeout
  or polling helper.
